      "text": "To solve for the largest value less than 500 that is common to both arithmetic progressions, we first define the general terms of each arithmetic progression.\n\nFor the first arithmetic progression, which is:\n\\[\n2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\n\\]\nthe \\(n\\)-th term is given by:\n\\[\na_n = 2 + (n-1) \\cdot 3\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\na_n = 3n - 1\n\\]\nThus, the \\(n\\)-th term of the first progression is \\(3n - 1\\).\n\nFor the second arithmetic progression, which is:\n\\[\n3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots\n\\]\nthe \\(n\\)-th term is given by:\n\\[\nb_n = 3 + (n-1) \\cdot 7\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\nb_n = 7n - 4\n\\]\nThus, the \\(n\\)-th term of the second progression is \\(7n - 4\\).\n\nWe want the common term, so we set \\(3n - 1 = 7m - 4\\) for some integers \\(n\\) and \\(m\\), meaning:\n\\[\n3n - 1 = 7m - 4 \\Rightarrow 3n - 7m = -3\n\\]\n\nThis is a system of linear conjugate equations. We solve it iteratively by finding the smallest \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) such that \\(3n - 7m = -3\\) \n\nFor \\(n = 4\\):\n\\[\n3(4) - 7(1) = 12 - 7 = 5 \\neq -3\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 7\\):\n\\[\n3(7) - 7(2) = 21 - 14 = 7 \\neq -3\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 10\\):\n\\[\n3(10) - 7(4) = 30 - 28 = 2 \\neq -3\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 3\\):\n\\[\n3(3) - 7(1) = 9 - 7 = 2 = -3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the values of \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) that fit the criterion are \\(n = 3\\) and \\(m = 1\\).\n\nThe corresponding common value is thus:\n\\[\na_3 = 3(3) - 1 = 8\n\\]\n\\[\n8 = 7 \\cdot 1 - 4\n\\]\n\nHence, the largest value less than 500 that is common to both arithmetic progressions is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{499}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify with code.\n```python\n# Function to compute the n-th term of the first arithmetic progression\ndef a_n(n):\n    return 3 * n - 1\n\n# Function to compute the n-th term of the second arithmetic progression\ndef b_n(n):\n    return 7 * n - 4\n\n# Iterative check for common terms less than 500\ndef common_value():\n    for n in range(1, 500):\n        a_n_value = a_n(n)\n        b_n_value = b_n(n)\n        if a_n_value < 500 and b_n_value < 500 and a_n_value == b_n_value:\n            return a_n_value\n\ncommon_value = common_value()\nprint(common_value)\n```",
